Enhancement: Add resumable placement scheduling and archival promotion

Vaultic now schedules pack placement from named metadata, recent-data,
archival-data, and cache policies. Missing placements are persisted in the
SlateDB `rq:` deadline queue with concrete target backends, retry state, and
non-zero overdue monitoring through `vaultic index placement`.

Successful backups enqueue placement work and perform one bounded best-effort
action without failing the backup when a placement backend is unavailable.
Operators can drain more work with `index placement --execute`; backend
bandwidth and request-rate limits defer excess work, and interrupted workers
resume from durable request and placement records.

Placement backends can declare physical locations, retrieval and cost hints,
minimum retention, request and bandwidth limits, and target pack sizes. Reads
prefer the cheapest live placement, warm only the selected backend, and fall
back to another live placement on failure. Eviction runs last and is refused if
the resulting placement set would violate copy, failure-domain, or offsite
requirements.

Surviving data is promoted by decrypting and repacking retained blobs into new,
archival-sized pack IDs on the archival backend. Successor publication records
blob locations, placement, typed lineage, and history atomically before the
source becomes delete-pending. A crash after publication resumes from lineage
without writing another replacement. Data that becomes unreachable before the
promotion crossover is never sent to archival storage.

